# The SELinux package set of the server profile.
#
# Nothing here is vendored. scripts/build-selinux.sh clones
# https://github.com/archlinuxhardened/selinux at the commit pinned below and
# builds exactly the directories named in this file, in this order. Overrides
# that this profile needs on top of that tree live in pkgbuilds/selinux-overrides/.
#
# Format: one package directory per line, in build order. `#` starts a comment;
# the comment block above each package is the reason it is in the set. A package
# that is NOT here is not built, and the "Not built" block at the bottom says why.
#
# Upstream pin. Move it deliberately, re-read the diff, and re-run the
# acceptance script before publishing.
commit=95e4d010acb572c454a4186d56b926f13c99c82c

# ── userland ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Additive: none of these replaces a stock Arch package, so installing them
# changes nothing about a machine that never enables SELinux.

# The policy database format itself. Everything below links it.
libsepol
# The library every SELinux-aware binary calls: is_selinux_enabled(),
# getcon()/setcon(), the file_contexts lookup. This is the library that the
# "-selinux" rebuilds are rebuilt against.
libselinux
# Compiles the policy language into a binary policy. Needed to build the policy
# package, and to compile a local .te module on the machine itself.
checkpolicy
# The CIL compiler. semodule links it to assemble the loaded policy from the
# installed modules, so it is not optional even when no .cil is written by hand.
secilc
# The policy query library and `sesearch`. A dependency of selinux-python, and
# the only way to answer "what does the policy actually allow" without reloading
# it. Read the note on selinux-python below before assuming it is cheap: these
# two together are 45 packages and 453 MiB on the target.
setools
# The management layer under semanage: reads/writes the policy store, runs
# setfiles after a module change.
libsemanage
# semodule_package / semodule_expand, used when building a local module from
# a .te or a .cil into a .pp for `semodule -i`.
semodule-utils
# `semodule`, `load_policy`, `setfiles`, `restorecon`, `fixfiles`, `setsebool`,
# `newrole`, and the pam_selinux the rebuilt pam links.
policycoreutils
# `semanage` (fcontext/port/login/boolean) and `audit2allow`. Without it a
# denial cannot be turned into a policy module on the machine, which is the
# whole permissive-to-enforcing workflow.
#
# THE BIGGEST OPEN QUESTION IN THIS SET. Measured on an installed machine:
# removing `setools` and `selinux-python`, and everything that came in with
# them, takes 45 packages and 452.9 MiB off the server -- 82% of this route's
# entire size cost. The reason is `setools` -> `python-networkx`, and Arch's
# python-networkx lists python-scipy, python-pandas, python-matplotlib and
# python-numpy as HARD dependencies. A headless server that turns SELinux on
# gets a scientific Python stack.
#
# They stay for now because this profile's setup leaf uses `semanage fcontext`,
# so dropping them is not a one-line edit. Splitting them into a second,
# optional addon is the recommendation in
# omarchy-server/reports/2026-08-29-mandatory-access-control.md.
selinux-python
# Relabels the files a pacman transaction just wrote. Without it every upgrade
# leaves new files with the label of their parent directory instead of the one
# file_contexts assigns, and the system drifts out of policy between relabels.
selinux-alpm-hook
# The policy. Modular reference policy with the Arch patches; this is the only
# policy the archlinuxhardened project maintains for Arch.
selinux-refpolicy-arch

# ── rebuilds ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Each of these `provides=`/`conflicts=` its stock counterpart, so installing
# it REPLACES a package the base already has. That is why the addon has to
# handle the replacement explicitly, and why the set is kept as small as a
# usable enforcing headless system allows.

# pam_selinux is what puts a login session into its own domain. Without it
# every session inherits the domain of whatever started it, which on a server
# means sshd's, and confinement of logged-in users does not exist.
pambase-selinux
pam-selinux
# `ls -Z`, `id -Z`, and — the part that matters beyond inspection — cp/mv/
# install preserving and setting contexts. Every install/ leaf of this profile
# writes files with `install`.
coreutils-selinux
# `login` (the serial and virtual console path) performs the same context
# transition sshd does, through pam_selinux; without this rebuild a console
# login lands unconfined. Also mount, which labels filesystems.
util-linux-selinux
# useradd/usermod label the home directory they create, and `semanage login`
# maps a Unix account to an SELinux user. A user created after the relabel
# would otherwise get an unlabeled home.
shadow-selinux
# sudo performs the role transition to the target user's domain. Unrebuilt,
# `sudo` keeps the caller's context and root-through-sudo is not distinguished
# from the caller in the audit trail.
sudo-selinux
# sshd calls setexeccon() before handing off to the user shell. This is the
# single most load-bearing rebuild on a headless machine: it is the only way in.
openssh-selinux
# systemd labels /run and /sys/fs/cgroup, honours the units' SELinuxContext=,
# transitions each service into its own domain at exec, and relabels /dev.
# Without it services all run in the domain init inherited and file labels
# under /run are wrong from the first boot.
systemd-selinux

# ── not built, and why ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# dbus-selinux, dbus-broker-selinux
#   dbus and dbus-broker are in the base (systemd pulls them), but the server
#   runs no bus client of its own: no NetworkManager, no logind session bus, no
#   desktop. Rebuilding them buys SELinux checks on bus messages nobody sends.
# findutils-selinux, psmisc-selinux, iproute2-selinux
#   `find -context`, `killall -Z`, `ip -Z`. Inspection conveniences, not
#   confinement. They cost three more package replacements each machine has to
#   keep in lockstep with Arch.
# logrotate-selinux, cronie-selinux
#   neither package is installed in the server core.
# mcstrans
#   translates MCS category labels for human readers. The policy this profile
#   loads is targeted, not MLS, so there is nothing to translate.
# restorecond
#   a daemon that watches a list of files and relabels them. A daemon and an
#   inotify watch to do what the alpm hook and `restorecon` already do on the
#   events that matter here.
# selinux-gui, selinux-sandbox, selinux-dbus-config
#   a GTK tool, an X sandbox and a bus policy file. All three are desktop.
# base-selinux, base-devel-selinux
#   meta packages that pull the whole set. This profile installs an explicit
#   list instead, because half of that set is not in the server core.
# sepolgen
#   the standalone package is the 2016 release, it still wants python2 to
#   build, and selinux-python `provides=sepolgen` while conflicting with
#   `sepolgen<2.7`. The generator that audit2allow calls lives in
#   selinux-python now.
# procps-ng
#   there is no procps-ng-selinux to build: archlinuxhardened does not maintain
#   one, and Arch's procps-ng does not link libselinux. `ps -Z` and `ps -o
#   label` are therefore unavailable; a process context is read from
#   /proc/PID/attr/current, which is what `omarchy-server-selinux status` does.
